    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Popcorn ceilings, technically known as acoustic ceilings and called Artex or textured ceilings in the UK, were a staple of mid-century residential construction. While they were originally designed to dampen sound and hide imperfections in the ceiling, today they are often viewed as an eyesore that dates a property and collects dust. Homeowners can have the texture professionally tested and removed or skimmed. For renters, permanent alteration is rarely an option. You are caught between living with an aesthetic you dislike and risking your deposit.</p>
<p>Fortunately, modern interior design and the “renter-friendly” movement offer several clever ways to disguise a popcorn ceiling without fixing anything to it. Whether you want a big transformation or a subtle distraction, these methods will help you reclaim your space. Start with safety, though, because it shapes every idea that follows.</p>

<h2 id="safety-considerations">Safety considerations</h2>
<p>Before you start any ceiling-hiding project, there are two critical factors to consider: <strong>asbestos</strong> and <strong>fire safety</strong>.</p>
<h3 id="asbestos">Asbestos</h3>
<p>Textured and popcorn ceiling coatings applied before 1999 in the UK (US guidance often cites ceilings from before the 1980s) may contain asbestos. You can’t tell by looking at a ceiling whether it does.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Don’t pierce, stick to, scrape, sand, drill or otherwise disturb the ceiling.</strong> That rules out tacks, staples, screws, adhesive hooks, tape and glued tiles. Even “gentle” fixings can release fibres or pull texture away.</li>
<li><strong>Ask your landlord or letting agent whether the ceiling has been tested.</strong> Anything fixed to the ceiling needs their written permission and, for older ceilings, testing first. Testing and any removal are the landlord’s responsibility and should be carried out by qualified professionals.</li>
<li>If the texture is already flaking or damaged, report it to your landlord in writing and leave it alone.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="fire-safety">Fire safety</h3>
<ul>
<li>Use <strong>inherently flame-retardant fabrics</strong> for anything hung overhead, and check the labels before you buy.</li>
<li>Keep fabric well clear of light fittings, bulbs, heaters, smoke alarms and sprinkler heads. Never cover or obstruct a smoke or heat alarm.</li>
<li>Check your tenancy agreement and any building fire rules, especially in flats.</li>
</ul>
<p>Every method below is designed to work without touching the ceiling at all.</p>

<h2 id="fabric-panels">1. Fabric panels</h2>
<p>Fabric is one of the most versatile ways to soften a large area. By hanging lightweight textiles such as linen, cotton or sheer voile below the ceiling, you create a “tented” effect that hides much of the popcorn texture and adds a layer of softness, making the room feel cosy and intentional.</p>
<p>The key is to support the fabric from something other than the ceiling:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>A freestanding frame</strong>, such as a four-poster or canopy bed frame, or a freestanding canopy over a reading nook, lets you drape fabric overhead with no ceiling contact.</li>
<li><strong>Tension rods fixed wall to wall</strong> (see below) can carry fabric in soft swags just below the ceiling.</li>
<li><strong>Existing curtain poles</strong> can hold a long panel that sweeps across a corner or alcove.</li>
</ul>
<p>Choose inherently flame-retardant fabric, keep it clear of the ceiling light and smoke alarm, and leave a generous gap rather than letting it rest against the texture.</p>

<h2 id="foam-tiles">2. Foam tiles: why we don’t recommend them</h2>
<p>Lightweight polystyrene ceiling tiles that mimic plasterwork or tin are often suggested online as a quick fix. We don’t recommend them for a rented home with a textured ceiling. Expanded polystyrene tiles are a recognised fire hazard, and sticking anything, even with tape or putty, to a popcorn ceiling risks disturbing a coating that may contain asbestos. Removing them can also pull texture away and cost you part of your deposit.</p>
<p>If you want that same overhead interest, a <strong>freestanding fabric canopy</strong> in a flame-retardant fabric gives a softer, safer result, and it comes with you when you move.</p>

<h2 id="tapestries">3. Tapestries</h2>
<p>If you want a quick fix that covers a good amount of space, tapestries are worth considering. Large wall hangings can be repurposed as overhead canopies, which works particularly well in bedrooms or maximalist living spaces.</p>
<p>Hang them the same way as fabric panels: from a bed canopy frame, a freestanding frame or tension rods between two walls, never pinned, stapled or stuck to the ceiling. To avoid a sagging look, support the tapestry on two or more rods so it sits fairly flat. Choose a light or neutral design to keep the room airy, or a bold pattern to turn the overhead space into a focal point. Check the label for flame-retardant properties, and keep it away from lights and alarms. This approach is easy to pack up when your tenancy ends.</p>

<h2 id="tension-rods">4. Tension rods</h2>
<p>For narrow rooms, hallways or specific sections of a larger room, tension rods are a genuinely no-drill solution. Fitted between two facing walls, slightly below the ceiling and not touching it, they can carry fabric “canopies” that hide much of the texture above.</p>
<p>This method needs no adhesives, nails or ceiling contact. You can use standard curtain panels, which come pre-hemmed and ready to slide onto the rods, to create a soft “drop ceiling” look. Choose rods rated for the span and weight, protect the wall paintwork at each end, and keep the fabric well clear of any ceiling light, smoke alarm or sprinkler.</p>

<h2 id="uplighting">5. Uplighting and smarter lighting</h2>
<p>Sometimes the best way to hide something is to change how you see it. Popcorn ceilings look worst when light skims across them at a shallow angle, because every bump casts a small shadow. That often happens with a single flush ceiling light, or an uplighter pushed right into a corner.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Switch off the flush ceiling light</strong> and use several lamps at furniture height instead. Lined or opaque shades keep light low and leave the ceiling in soft shadow.</li>
<li><strong>If you use an uplighter, move it away from the wall</strong>, at least 60–90 cm (2–3 ft) towards the middle of the room, so the light meets the ceiling more directly and evenly rather than grazing it.</li>
<li><strong>Choose warm, diffused bulbs.</strong> Frosted or opal bulbs around 2700K soften the light; bright cool-white and clear filament bulbs sharpen texture.</li>
<li><strong>Soft LED strips</strong> on top of tall wardrobes or bookcases can give a gentle, diffused glow, but test them at night. If the light skims the ceiling and shows the texture, turn them down or point them away.</li>
</ul>
<p>This doesn’t physically hide the ceiling, but it makes the texture fade into the background so your furniture and decor take centre stage. Our <a href="/blog/layered-lighting-room-by-room-guide">layered lighting guide</a> explains how to balance lamps room by room.</p>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Check the room at night with each lamp on in turn. Whichever one makes the ceiling look rough is either too close to a wall or pointing too far upwards.</p></blockquote>
<!--figure:living-room-shelves-paper-lanterns|Paper lantern pendants, open shelves and a cream sofa keep the eye at furniture height in a relaxed living room.-->

<h2 id="contact-paper">6. Contact paper</h2>
<p>You’ll see ideas online for wood-grain contact paper “beams” or contact-paper-covered boards tacked to the ceiling. Don’t apply contact paper to a popcorn ceiling, and don’t tack or stick boards to it: both involve fixing to a surface that may contain asbestos, and removal can pull the texture away.</p>
<p>Contact paper is still useful lower down, where it helps draw the eye away from the ceiling. Use it to refresh shelf backs, the top of a side table or a plain sideboard, so the interesting surfaces sit at eye level. Test it on a hidden spot first and check that it will peel off cleanly.</p>

<h2 id="faux-beams">7. Faux beams</h2>
<p>Faux wood beams, usually made of lightweight high-density polyurethane, can make a room look architectural, and running two or three across a ceiling breaks up the view so the texture between them reads as background. They have to be fixed to the ceiling, though, so in a rental they are only an option with your landlord’s written permission, after the ceiling has been tested for asbestos, and with professional fixing.</p>
<p>For most renters it’s simpler to skip them and create the same sense of structure at eye level: large art, a strong rug and low, horizontal furniture all pull the eye down.</p>
<!--figure:living-room-abstract-art|A large abstract painting above a curved sofa gives the eye a strong focal point at seating height.-->

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>Hiding a popcorn ceiling in a rental takes a blend of creativity and caution. <strong>Fabric panels and tapestries</strong> hung from freestanding frames or tension rods give soft, bohemian coverage without touching the ceiling. For those who want to avoid the ceiling altogether, <strong>better lighting</strong> is the most effective psychological trick in the book, helped by eye-level art and furniture. Whatever you choose, ask your landlord about asbestos testing, leave the ceiling itself alone and use flame-retardant fabrics, and you’ll give yourself the best chance of getting your deposit back in full.</p>

<h2 id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3 id="mounting-damage">Will mounting things to the ceiling damage the popcorn texture?</h3>
<p>It can, and that’s why we don’t recommend it. Popcorn texture is often brittle, and adhesives, tacks or staples can pull pieces away or create dust. On older ceilings that may contain asbestos, disturbing the surface is a health risk, not just a deposit risk. Anything fixed to the ceiling needs your landlord’s written permission and, for older ceilings, testing first.</p>
<h3 id="painting-popcorn-ceiling">Can I paint a popcorn ceiling to hide it?</h3>
<p>Painting doesn’t hide the texture; it can make it more prominent and harder to remove in future. Most landlords treat painting a ceiling as a permanent change that needs their permission, and it isn’t advised on a ceiling that may contain asbestos, as wet paint and rollers can disturb the coating.</p>
<h3 id="fabric-safe">Is it safe to cover a ceiling with fabric?</h3>
<p>It can be, if the fabric is hung from a freestanding frame, tension rods or existing poles rather than fixed to the ceiling. Use inherently flame-retardant fabric (check the label), keep it away from lights, heaters and other heat sources, and leave smoke alarms and sprinkler heads completely clear.</p>
<h3 id="cost">How much does it cost to cover a popcorn ceiling?</h3>
<p>Costs vary with room size and materials. Lighting changes, such as moving lamps or swapping bulbs and shades, are usually the most affordable option, followed by freestanding fabric or tension rods with curtain panels. Anything that requires professional fixing, such as faux beams installed with landlord permission after testing, is the most expensive route.</p>
<h3 id="cleaning">How do I clean a ceiling cover?</h3>
<p>If you use fabric or tapestries, take them down and wash them according to the care label once or twice a year. Washing can affect some flame-retardant finishes, so check the label for guidance. Between washes, a gentle vacuum with an upholstery attachment keeps dust down.</p>

